What are Electrostatic Chucks (ESCs)?
Electrostatic Chucks (ESCs) are critical components in semiconductor manufacturing that utilize electrostatic force to securely hold silicon wafers during processes like etching, deposition, and ion implantation. These devices eliminate mechanical clamping, reducing contamination risks while ensuring precise wafer positioning and temperature control. The technology relies on high-voltage electric fields generated by embedded electrodes to create an electrostatic attraction between the chuck surface and wafer.

With global production reaching 57,358 units in 2025 at an average price of USD 23,000 per unit, ESCs represent a specialized but essential segment of semiconductor equipment. Key manufacturers are focusing on material innovations and integrated solutions to address evolving industry requirements for yield improvement and thermal management.

